}# General d-position sets — verified generator for arXiv:2005.08095

| Profile field | Value |
|---|---|
| Track | **A — structural hardness** |
| Native domain | combinatorics |
| Object regime | finite discrete |
| Computational core | graph |
| Certificate form | integer tuple |
| Intuition | reduction recognition |
| Domain essentiality | licensed reduction |
| Reduction | paper-licensed, Section 2 Proposition 2.1 and Theorem 2.2 |

## What the problem is

The source is Klavžar, Rall, and Yero, [“General d-position sets”](https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.08095). A set of graph vertices is in general `d`-position when no three distinct selected vertices occur on one shortest path of length at most `d`. The solver is handed the paper's graph `G'`: a compactly specified `H_t` joined to a colored compatibility graph `G`. It must name one vertex from every color so that `A ∪ B` together with those vertices is a general `d`-position set of the prescribed size. The checker needs only exact adjacency tests: the expansion is a clique precisely when all submitted cross-color pairs are edges, and a clique cannot contain a three-vertex geodesic.

This is the paper's central reduction, not an independently invented graph surrogate. Theorem 2.2 constructs exactly this (G') and proves

\[
\operatorname{gp}_d(G')=\operatorname{gp}_d(H_t)+\omega(G).
\]

The answer is generated first. Every color-pair graph is then made regular and conditioned to contain the corresponding planted edge. No clique or general-position instance is solved during generation.

## Why this Track A claim is plausible—and its limit

Theorem 2.2 proves NP-completeness by reduction from Maximum Clique for every `d ≥ 2`. The generator uses the theorem at `d = t = diam(G')`, with 22 color classes of size 32 and 23-regular bipartite compatibility graphs. The degree is near the first-moment threshold `n^k (degree/n)^(k choose 2) ≈ 1`, so an unconditioned instance has roughly one full transversal clique in expectation while construction guarantees one.

At the shipping preset, exact multicolored-clique search with minimum-remaining-values branching and forward checking exhausted 500,000 nodes on 8/8 seeds. It averaged 8,716,471 compatibility updates and 52.48 wall-clock seconds on the contended build host. Triangle-outlier, greedy, 64-restart, and centered spectral attacks also failed on all eight seeds.

The paper proves worst-case hardness, not average-case hardness for this planted regular distribution. The Track A distribution claim therefore rests on the measured adversary panel and the absence of a known direct recovery rule, not on a distributional theorem in the paper. That distinction is important.

## Worked demo

The demo (`seed=0`) is hand-solvable: inspect the three 3×3 matrices and choose one mutually adjacent label from each class.

```text
Find a prescribed-size general d-position set in a graph given by a compact recipe.

Definitions.
For vertices x,y, dist(x,y) is the minimum number of edges on an x-y path.
A geodesic is a path whose length equals that distance. A set S is in
general d-position when no three distinct vertices of S occur on one
geodesic of length at most d.

There are k=3 colour classes C1,...,C3, each containing n=3
vertices. Their disjoint union is a graph G of order t=9. There are no
edges inside a colour class. Between each pair of classes, edges are given
by the compatibility matrices below.

The full graph G' is the following Section 2 construction.
1. Make H_t. Its sets A={a1,...,a18} and B={b1,...,b18}
   together induce a complete graph K_36.
2. Add the path v1-v2-...-v8. Join every vertex of B to v1.
3. For every i from 2 through 8, add u_i adjacent to v_i and v_(i-1).
   There are no other H_t edges.
4. Take the disjoint union of H_t and G, then join every G vertex to every
   vertex of A, every vertex of B, and v1. Add no other cross edges.
The resulting G' has 60 vertices. Here d=t=9.

Your compact answer must give exactly one vertex label from each C_i, in
the order C1,...,C3. The checker expands it to S=A union B union your
3 submitted vertices and checks the graph conditions. The required
expanded-set size is exactly 39.
Do not output the A or B vertices themselves.

Matrix convention.
For PAIR Ci Cj with i<j, rows follow the printed order of Ci and bit
positions follow the printed order of Cj. Bit 1 means an edge; bit 0 means
no edge. Positions in this explanation are 1-based. Slash-separated bit
strings are consecutive rows. Decimal labels are arbitrary identifiers.

Colour classes (in answer-slot order):
  C1: 1 2 9
  C2: 3 6 7
  C3: 5 8 4

Compatibility matrices:
  PAIR C1 C2: 011/110/101
  PAIR C1 C3: 101/110/011
  PAIR C2 C3: 101/110/011

Output exactly 3 distinct decimal labels as a JSON list. Entry i must
belong to Ci. Order is significant, repeats are forbidden, and all displayed
bounds are inclusive.

Give your final answer inside <answer></answer> tags, as that JSON list.
Example syntax only: <answer>[1,3,5]</answer>
Output nothing else inside the tags.
```

The planted answer is `[2, 6, 5]`; `verify(inst, [2, 6, 5])` returns `(True, "ok")`. The corrupted answer `[1, 3, 5]` returns `(False, "labels 1 and 3 are incompatible for colours 1 and 2")`. There are 9 valid answers among the demo's 27 structurally admissible candidates.

