-## What the problem is
+## What the family is
 
-The source is Klavžar, Rall, and Yero, [“General d-position sets”](https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.08095). A set of graph vertices is in general `d`-position when no three distinct selected vertices occur on one shortest path of length at most `d`. The solver is handed the paper's graph `G'`: a compactly specified `H_t` joined to a colored compatibility graph `G`. It must name one vertex from every color so that `A ∪ B` together with those vertices is a general `d`-position set of the prescribed size. The checker needs only exact adjacency tests: the expansion is a clique precisely when all submitted cross-color pairs are edges, and a clique cannot contain a three-vertex geodesic.
+The source is Klavžar, Rall, and Yero, [“General d-position sets”](https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.08095). A vertex set is in general `d`-position when no three selected vertices occur on one shortest path of length at most `d`.
 
-This is the paper's central reduction, not an independently invented graph surrogate. Theorem 2.2 constructs exactly this (G') and proves
+The solver receives the paper's graph `G'` in an exact compact form. Its graph `G` has `k` colour classes over the prime field `F_p`; a pair relation is the union of three affine perfect matchings. The paper's `H_t` is joined to `G` exactly as in Theorem 2.2. The solver names one vertex per colour so that the selected `G`-vertices form a clique. The checker expands the answer by the fixed clique `A ∪ B`; the expansion is itself a clique, so exact modular adjacency checks directly establish the geodesic condition. No optimality oracle is used.
 
+Generation is inverse. It first samples relative shifts and three high field roots, then places the required affine intercept in every relation. Decoy intercepts are resampled only until the public cocycle has a unique consistent branch. The answer is never obtained by solving the finished instance. The construction has exactly three valid transversal witnesses.
+
+## Why this is Track B
+
+Section 2, Theorem 2.2 proves NP-completeness for every `d ≥ 2` by the identity
+
 \[
 \operatorname{gp}_d(G')=\operatorname{gp}_d(H_t)+\omega(G).
 \]
 
-The answer is generated first. Every color-pair graph is then made regular and conditioned to contain the corresponding planted edge. No clique or general-position instance is solved during generation.
-
-## Why this Track A claim is plausible—and its limit
-
-Theorem 2.2 proves NP-completeness by reduction from Maximum Clique for every `d ≥ 2`. The generator uses the theorem at `d = t = diam(G')`, with 22 color classes of size 32 and 23-regular bipartite compatibility graphs. The degree is near the first-moment threshold `n^k (degree/n)^(k choose 2) ≈ 1`, so an unconditioned instance has roughly one full transversal clique in expectation while construction guarantees one.
+This module does **not** turn that worst-case theorem into an average-case Track A claim. An efficient algorithm exists for this generated distribution and is reported openly. Lexicographic multicoloured-clique search with forward checking costs `O(p r² + k r²)` here (`r=3`). At shipping size it solved 8/8 instances, averaging 181,009.5 scanned field values, 7,060,503 counted operations, and 0.616 seconds on this host.
 
-At the shipping preset, exact multicolored-clique search with minimum-remaining-values branching and forward checking exhausted 500,000 nodes on 8/8 seeds. It averaged 8,716,471 compatibility updates and 52.48 wall-clock seconds on the contended build host. Triangle-outlier, greedy, 64-restart, and centered spectral attacks also failed on all eight seeds.
+The compact route is much shorter. Among ordinary slope-1 relations, the planted offsets are the unique choices satisfying `δ_ij + δ_jk = δ_ik`. Two anchor colours determine every relative shift; the single slope-2 relation then determines the global translation. The implemented route independently reconstructed and verified a witness in 199 exact arithmetic operations. That 7-million-versus-199 gap is the Track B hardness claim: the mechanical route is easy for a program but not executable by hand in context.
 
-The paper proves worst-case hardness, not average-case hardness for this planted regular distribution. The Track A distribution claim therefore rests on the measured adversary panel and the absence of a known direct recovery rule, not on a distributional theorem in the paper. That distinction is important.
+The easy regimes in the paper were avoided. Propositions 3.1 and 3.3 give closed formulas for paths and cycles. Proposition 5.3 identifies triangle-free general 2-position with dissociation sets, and Section 6 notes polynomial algorithms for the relevant tree cases.
 
 ## Worked demo
 
