-## What the problem is
+## What the family is
 
-The source is Klavžar, Rall, and Yero, [“General d-position sets”](https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.08095). A set of graph vertices is in general `d`-position when no three distinct selected vertices occur on one shortest path of length at most `d`. The solver is handed the paper's graph `G'`: a compactly specified `H_t` joined to a colored compatibility graph `G`. It must name one vertex from every color so that `A ∪ B` together with those vertices is a general `d`-position set of the prescribed size. The checker needs only exact adjacency tests: the expansion is a clique precisely when all submitted cross-color pairs are edges, and a clique cannot contain a three-vertex geodesic.
+The source is Klavžar, Rall, and Yero, [“General d-position sets”](https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.08095). A vertex set is in general `d`-position when no three selected vertices occur on one shortest path of length at most `d`.
 
-This is the paper's central reduction, not an independently invented graph surrogate. Theorem 2.2 constructs exactly this (G') and proves
+The solver receives the paper's graph `G'` in an exact compact form. Its graph `G` has `k` colour classes over the prime field `F_p`; a pair relation is the union of three affine perfect matchings. The paper's `H_t` is joined to `G` exactly as in Theorem 2.2. The solver names one vertex per colour so that the selected `G`-vertices form a clique. The checker expands the answer by the fixed clique `A ∪ B`; the expansion is itself a clique, so exact modular adjacency checks directly establish the geodesic condition. No optimality oracle is used.
 
+Generation is inverse. It first samples relative shifts and three uniformly random field roots, then places the required affine intercept in every relation. Decoy intercepts are resampled only until the public cocycle has a unique consistent branch. Plants and decoys use the same field distribution, and the answer is never obtained by solving the finished instance. The construction has exactly three valid transversal witnesses.
+
+## Why this is Track B
+
+Section 2, Theorem 2.2 proves NP-completeness for every `d ≥ 2` by the identity
+
 \[
 \operatorname{gp}_d(G')=\operatorname{gp}_d(H_t)+\omega(G).
 \]
 
-The answer is generated first. Every color-pair graph is then made regular and conditioned to contain the corresponding planted edge. No clique or general-position instance is solved during generation.
