@@ -1,179 +0,0 @@
-# General d-position sets — verified generator for arXiv:2005.08095
-
-| Profile field | Value |
-|---|---|
-| Track | **B — no-tool compression** |
-| Native domain | combinatorics |
-| Object regime | finite discrete |
-| Computational core | graph |
-| Certificate form | integer tuple |
-| Intended intuition | invariant: an additive cocycle plus one affine constraint |
-| Domain essentiality | licensed reduction |
-| Reduction | paper-licensed, Section 2 Proposition 2.1 and Theorem 2.2 |
-
-## What the family is
-
-The source is Klavžar, Rall, and Yero, [“General d-position sets”](https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.08095). A vertex set is in general `d`-position when no three selected vertices occur on one shortest path of length at most `d`.
-
-The solver receives the paper's graph `G'` in an exact compact form. Its graph `G` has `k` colour classes over the prime field `F_p`; a pair relation is the union of three affine perfect matchings. The paper's `H_t` is joined to `G` exactly as in Theorem 2.2. The solver names one vertex per colour so that the selected `G`-vertices form a clique. The checker expands the answer by the fixed clique `A ∪ B`; the expansion is itself a clique, so exact modular adjacency checks directly establish the geodesic condition. No optimality oracle is used.
-
-Generation is inverse. It first samples relative shifts and three uniformly random field roots, then places the required affine intercept in every relation. Decoy intercepts are resampled only until the public cocycle has a unique consistent branch. Plants and decoys use the same field distribution, and the answer is never obtained by solving the finished instance. The construction has exactly three valid transversal witnesses.
-
-## Why this is Track B
-
-Section 2, Theorem 2.2 proves NP-completeness for every `d ≥ 2` by the identity
-
-\[
-\operatorname{gp}_d(G')=\operatorname{gp}_d(H_t)+\omega(G).
-\]
-
-This module does **not** turn that worst-case theorem into an average-case Track A claim. An efficient algorithm exists for this generated distribution and is reported openly. Lexicographic multicoloured-clique search with forward checking costs `O(p r² + k r²)` here (`r=3`). At shipping size it solved 8/8 instances, averaging 48,057 scanned field values, 1,875,091 counted operations, and 0.163 seconds on this host.
-
-The compact route is much shorter. Among ordinary slope-1 relations, the planted offsets are the unique choices satisfying `δ_ij + δ_jk = δ_ik`. Two anchor colours determine every relative shift; the single slope-2 relation then determines the global translation. The implemented route independently reconstructed and verified a witness in 199 exact arithmetic operations. That 1.88-million-versus-199 gap is the Track B hardness claim: the mechanical route is easy for a program but not executable by hand in context.
-
-The easy regimes in the paper were avoided. Propositions 3.1 and 3.3 give closed formulas for paths and cycles. Proposition 5.3 identifies triangle-free general 2-position with dissociation sets, and Section 6 notes polynomial algorithms for the relevant tree cases.
-
-## Worked demo
-
-The demo (`seed=0`) is hand-solvable. Its full rendered statement is:
-
-```text
-Find a prescribed-size general d-position set in a compactly specified graph.
-
-Definitions.
-For vertices x,y, dist(x,y) is the minimum number of edges on an x-y path.
-A geodesic is a path whose length equals that distance. A set S is in
-general d-position when no three distinct vertices of S occur on one
-geodesic of length at most d.
-
-Let p=17, a prime. Arithmetic below is modulo p, with residues 0,...,16.
-The graph G has k=4 colour classes C1,...,C4. Class Ci (with i
-1-based) contains the p integer vertex labels (i-1)*p through i*p-1.
-The residue coordinate of a label in Ci is label-(i-1)*p.
-There are no edges inside a colour class.
-
-For each line PAIR Ci Cj: a ; b1,b2,b3, where i<j, a vertex with
-residue x in Ci is adjacent to a vertex with residue y in Cj exactly when
-y = a*x+b modulo p for at least one listed b. All three b values are
-included, and there are no other G-edges.
-
-Pair relations:
-  PAIR C1 C2: 1 ; 5,11,8
-  PAIR C1 C3: 2 ; 9,14,15
-  PAIR C1 C4: 1 ; 3,7,1
-  PAIR C2 C3: 1 ; 2,12,1
-  PAIR C2 C4: 1 ; 16,3,6
-  PAIR C3 C4: 1 ; 4,11,3
-
-The full graph G' is the following Section 2 construction.
-1. Make H_t for t=68. Its sets A={a1,...,a136} and
-   B={b1,...,b136} together induce a complete graph K_272.
-2. Add the path v1-v2-...-v67, and join every B vertex to v1.
-3. For i=2,...,67, add u_i adjacent to v_i and v_(i-1).
-   These are all H_t edges.
-4. Join every G vertex to every vertex of A, every vertex of B, and v1.
-   Add no other cross edges.
-Thus G' has 473 vertices, and d=t=68.
-
-Submit exactly one G-vertex label from each C_i, in C1,...,C4 order.
-The checker expands the answer to S=A union B union those 4 vertices.
-The required expanded size is exactly 276. Do not list
-the A or B vertices. Order is significant; bounds are inclusive.
-
-Output exactly 4 distinct decimal labels as a JSON list.
-Give your final answer inside <answer></answer> tags, as that JSON list.
-Example syntax only: <answer>[0,17,34,51]</answer>
-Output nothing else inside the tags.
-```
-
-The planted answer `[6, 31, 43, 64]` gives `verify(...) == (True, "ok")`. Replacing its first entry by zero gives `(False, "labels 0 and 31 are incompatible for colours 1 and 2")`. The independent cocycle route finds another valid answer, `[11, 19, 48, 52]`, in 37 arithmetic operations. A person can carry out those few modular checks on paper.
-
-## Difficulty presets
-
