-## What the family is
+## What the family is, and whether to trust it
+
+The source is Klavžar, Rall, and Yero, [“General d-position sets”](https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.08095). A set is in general `d`-position when no three of its vertices occur on one shortest path of length at most `d`.
 
-The source is Klavžar, Rall, and Yero, [“General d-position sets”](https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.08095). A vertex set is in general `d`-position when no three selected vertices occur on one shortest path of length at most `d`.
+The solver receives the paper's graph `G'` in an exact compact representation. Its payload graph `G` has `k` colour classes over the prime field `F_p`; every colour-pair relation is a union of three affine perfect matchings. The graph `H_t` is joined to `G` exactly as in Theorem 2.2. The requested answer is one payload vertex per colour. The checker expands it by the fixed clique `A ∪ B`. That expanded set is in general `d`-position exactly when the submitted payload vertices form a clique: a missing payload edge puts any selected `A` vertex on a length-two geodesic, while a clique has no selected vertex between two others. Verification is therefore an exact cardinality, range, and modular-adjacency check.
 
-The solver receives the paper's graph `G'` in an exact compact form. Its graph `G` has `k` colour classes over the prime field `F_p`; a pair relation is the union of three affine perfect matchings. The paper's `H_t` is joined to `G` exactly as in Theorem 2.2. The solver names one vertex per colour so that the selected `G`-vertices form a clique. The checker expands the answer by the fixed clique `A ∪ B`; the expansion is itself a clique, so exact modular adjacency checks directly establish the geodesic condition. No optimality oracle is used.
+Generation is inverse. It samples the transversal and three global field roots first, builds every pair relation around them, and retains only instances with a unique public cocycle branch. The exceptional affine relation leaves exactly the three sampled roots, so there are exactly three valid witnesses. The answer is never obtained by solving the finished instance.
 
-Generation is inverse. It first samples relative shifts and three uniformly random field roots, then places the required affine intercept in every relation. Decoy intercepts are resampled only until the public cocycle has a unique consistent branch. Plants and decoys use the same field distribution, and the answer is never obtained by solving the finished instance. The construction has exactly three valid transversal witnesses.
+All local correctness gates pass. **The revised family is not yet release-trusted:** the required oracle calls currently return HTTP 403 “Key limit exceeded” from every configured vendor, leaving zero usable attempts. The included current transcripts record those service errors and are not hardness evidence. Older evidence for the visibly slope-1 version is preserved under `previous_v1_*`; that version was easy for the oracles and is not the present generator.
 
 ## Why this is Track B
 
-Section 2, Theorem 2.2 proves NP-completeness for every `d ≥ 2` by the identity
+Theorem 2.2 proves NP-completeness for every `d ≥ 2` through
 
 \[
 \operatorname{gp}_d(G')=\operatorname{gp}_d(H_t)+\omega(G).
 \]
 
-This module does **not** turn that worst-case theorem into an average-case Track A claim. An efficient algorithm exists for this generated distribution and is reported openly. Lexicographic multicoloured-clique search with forward checking costs `O(p r² + k r²)` here (`r=3`). At shipping size it solved 8/8 instances, averaging 48,057 scanned field values, 1,875,091 counted operations, and 0.163 seconds on this host.
+This module does not turn worst-case NP-completeness into an average-case claim. A complete reference algorithm exists: lexicographic multicoloured-clique backtracking. Its worst-case cost is `O(p k² r^(k-1))`, and with fixed shipping `k=20`, `r=3` it scales linearly in `p`. At `p=200003` it solved 8/8 audit instances, averaging 82,737.75 scanned field values, 4,220,421 counted exact operations, and 0.297 seconds on this host.
 
-The compact route is much shorter. Among ordinary slope-1 relations, the planted offsets are the unique choices satisfying `δ_ij + δ_jk = δ_ik`. Two anchor colours determine every relative shift; the single slope-2 relation then determines the global translation. The implemented route independently reconstructed and verified a witness in 199 exact arithmetic operations. That 1.88-million-versus-199 gap is the Track B hardness claim: the mechanical route is easy for a program but not executable by hand in context.
+The intended compression uses the public representation. Ordinary pair slopes satisfy a multiplicative cocycle; the two failed triangles among the first four colours identify the exceptional pair. The matching intercepts then satisfy a twisted additive cocycle. Recovering that branch and solving the one exceptional affine equation takes at most 275 counted operations over the eight audit seeds, including every Euclidean division used for modular inversion. Independent affine coordinates hide the obvious all-slope-1 presentation.
 
-The easy regimes in the paper were avoided. Propositions 3.1 and 3.3 give closed formulas for paths and cycles. Proposition 5.3 identifies triangle-free general 2-position with dissociation sets, and Section 6 notes polynomial algorithms for the relevant tree cases.
