     1	# General d-position sets — generator for arXiv:2005.08095
     2	
     3	| Profile field | Value |
     4	|---|---|
     5	| Track | **B — no-tool compression** |
     6	| Native domain | combinatorics |
     7	| Object regime | finite discrete |
     8	| Computational core | graph |
     9	| Certificate form | integer tuple |
    10	| Intended intuition | invariant: multiplicative slopes and twisted additive intercepts |
    11	| Domain essentiality | licensed reduction |
    12	| Reduction | paper-licensed, Section 2, Proposition 2.1 and Theorem 2.2 |
    13	
    14	## What the family is, and whether to trust it
    15	
    16	The source is Klavžar, Rall, and Yero, [“General d-position sets”](https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.08095). A set is in general `d`-position when no three of its vertices occur on one shortest path of length at most `d`.
    17	
    18	The solver receives the paper's graph `G'` in an exact compact representation. Its payload graph `G` has `k` colour classes over the prime field `F_p`; every colour-pair relation is a union of three affine perfect matchings. The graph `H_t` is joined to `G` exactly as in Theorem 2.2. The requested answer is one payload vertex per colour. The checker expands it by the fixed clique `A ∪ B`. That expanded set is in general `d`-position exactly when the submitted payload vertices form a clique: a missing payload edge puts any selected `A` vertex on a length-two geodesic, while a clique has no selected vertex between two others. Verification is therefore an exact cardinality, range, and modular-adjacency check.
    19	
    20	Generation is inverse. It samples the transversal and three global field roots first, builds every pair relation around them, and retains only instances with a unique public cocycle branch. The exceptional affine relation leaves exactly the three sampled roots, so there are exactly three valid witnesses. The answer is never obtained by solving the finished instance.
    21	
    22	All local correctness gates pass. **The revised family is not yet release-trusted:** the required oracle calls currently return HTTP 403 “Key limit exceeded” from every configured vendor, leaving zero usable attempts. The included current transcripts record those service errors and are not hardness evidence. Older evidence for the visibly slope-1 version is preserved under `previous_v1_*`; that version was easy for the oracles and is not the present generator.
    23	
    24	## Why this is Track B
    25	
    26	Theorem 2.2 proves NP-completeness for every `d ≥ 2` through
    27	
    28	\[
    29	\operatorname{gp}_d(G')=\operatorname{gp}_d(H_t)+\omega(G).
    30	\]
    31	
    32	This module does not turn worst-case NP-completeness into an average-case claim. A complete reference algorithm exists: lexicographic multicoloured-clique backtracking. Its worst-case cost is `O(p k² r^(k-1))`, and with fixed shipping `k=20`, `r=3` it scales linearly in `p`. At `p=200003` it solved 8/8 audit instances, averaging 82,737.75 scanned field values, 4,220,421 counted exact operations, and about 0.30 seconds on this host.
    33	
    34	The intended compression uses the public representation. Ordinary pair slopes satisfy a multiplicative cocycle; the two failed triangles among the first four colours identify the exceptional pair. The matching intercepts then satisfy a twisted additive cocycle. Recovering that branch and solving the one exceptional affine equation takes at most 275 counted operations over the eight audit seeds, including every Euclidean division used for modular inversion. Independent affine coordinates hide the obvious all-slope-1 presentation.
    35	
    36	The paper's easy regimes were avoided: Propositions 3.1 and 3.3 give formulas for paths and cycles; Proposition 5.3 equates general 2-position and dissociation sets on triangle-free graphs; Section 6 notes polynomial algorithms for the relevant tree cases.
    37	
    38	## Worked demo
    39	
    40	The demo at `seed=0` is hand-solvable and renders as follows.
    41	
    42	```text
    43	Find a prescribed-size general d-position set in a compactly specified graph.
    44	
    45	Definitions.
    46	For vertices x,y, dist(x,y) is the minimum number of edges on an x-y path.
    47	A geodesic is a path whose length equals that distance. A set S is in
    48	general d-position when no three distinct vertices of S occur on one
    49	geodesic of length at most d.
    50	
    51	Let p=17, a prime. Arithmetic below is modulo p, with residues 0,...,16.
    52	The graph G has k=4 colour classes C1,...,C4. Class Ci (with i
    53	1-based) contains the p integer vertex labels (i-1)*p through i*p-1.
    54	The residue coordinate of a label in Ci is label-(i-1)*p.
    55	There are no edges inside a colour class.
    56	
    57	For each line PAIR Ci Cj: a ; b1,b2,b3, where i<j, a vertex with
    58	residue x in Ci is adjacent to a vertex with residue y in Cj exactly when
    59	y = a*x+b modulo p for at least one listed b. All three b values are
    60	included, and there are no other G-edges.
    61	
    62	Pair relations:
    63	  PAIR C1 C2: 7 ; 16,14,12
    64	  PAIR C1 C3: 3 ; 6,2,11
    65	  PAIR C1 C4: 15 ; 16,14,7
    66	  PAIR C2 C3: 16 ; 8,10,12
    67	  PAIR C2 C4: 7 ; 13,9,15
    68	  PAIR C3 C4: 10 ; 13,3,10
    69	
    70	The full graph G' is the following Section 2 construction.
    71	1. Make H_t for t=68. Its sets A={a1,...,a136} and
    72	   B={b1,...,b136} together induce a complete graph K_272.
    73	2. Add the path v1-v2-...-v67, and join every B vertex to v1.
    74	3. For i=2,...,67, add u_i adjacent to v_i and v_(i-1).
    75	   These are all H_t edges.
    76	4. Join every G vertex to every vertex of A, every vertex of B, and v1.
    77	   Add no other cross edges.
    78	Thus G' has 473 vertices, and d=t=68.
    79	
    80	Submit exactly one G-vertex label from each C_i, in C1,...,C4 order.
    81	The checker expands the answer to S=A union B union those 4 vertices.
    82	The required expanded size is exactly 276. Do not list
    83	the A or B vertices. Order is significant; bounds are inclusive.
    84	
    85	Output exactly 4 distinct decimal labels as a JSON list.
    86	Give your final answer inside <answer></answer> tags, as that JSON list.
    87	Example syntax only: <answer>[0,17,34,51]</answer>
    88	Output nothing else inside the tags.
    89	```
    90	
    91	`[16, 22, 37, 67]` verifies as `(True, "ok")`. Changing the first label to zero gives `(False, "labels 0 and 22 are incompatible for colours 1 and 2")`. A person can solve this four-colour example by checking the six small modular relations.
