Implemented a Track B generator using the paper’s Theorem 2.2 Maximum Clique reduction. The construction is inverse-generated, has exactly three valid witnesses, and verifies using exact modular arithmetic. [Paper](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2005.08095)

Files:

- [Generator](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2005.08095/gen_2005_08095.py)
- [Self-test report](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2005.08095/selftest_report.json)
- [README](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2005.08095/README.md)
- [Bare transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2005.08095/llm_loop_transcript.jsonl)
- [Hinted transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2005.08095/g9_hinted_transcript.jsonl)
- [Placebo transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2005.08095/g9_placebo_transcript.jsonl)

All local gates pass. At shipping size:

- Guessing: 0/200,000
- Reference solver: 4,220,421 mean operations
- Compact route: at most 275 operations
- Answer: 20 atoms, 155 characters
- Adversary panel: 0/8 successes for all four attacks

The required oracle hardening remains blocked: both configured OpenRouter vendors returned HTTP 403 “Key limit exceeded,” yielding zero usable attempts. The transcripts record these as service errors, not model failures. The README consequently marks the generator as not yet release-ready; rerun `harden.py` after restoring quota.
